<p>
  A standard insulation tester is built for one test voltage — a CIE/444 configured for 500 V testing does 500 V testing, reliably, for its whole service life. A multirange insulation tester does the same job across several selectable test voltages from a single instrument. For a maintenance store or a contractor working across many equipment types, that difference decides how many testers you actually need to own. This guide explains what a multirange tester is and when it earns its higher cost over a fixed-voltage unit.
</p>

<h2 id="the-problem">The Problem Multirange Solves</h2>

<p>
  Different equipment classes call for different insulation test voltages — roughly 500 V for LV motors and cables, 1000–2500 V for MV equipment, up to 5000 V or beyond for HV apparatus (see our <a href={u('/blog/what-is-a-megger/')}>megger guide</a> for the full test-voltage table). A maintenance team that services LV panels one day and MV switchgear the next either carries multiple fixed-voltage testers, or carries one multirange instrument that covers the whole span with a selector switch.
</p>

<h2 id="how-it-works">How a Multirange Tester Selects Voltage</h2>

<p>
  A multirange hand-driven tester still generates its high voltage from the same crank-driven generator principle as a fixed-voltage unit, but routes the output through a selectable voltage-configuration stage — a series of pre-set combinations the operator dials in before testing, rather than the single fixed configuration of a standard model. Each series designation corresponds to a specific set of available test voltages, so specifying the right series when ordering matters as much as specifying the model itself.
</p>

<Callout type="tip" title="The series designation is not optional detail">
  CIE's hand-driven multirange testers are ordered by series — MR-1 through MR-4 on the CIE/444/MR, or up to MR-8 on the CIE/666/MR for a wider spread of high-voltage configurations — and the motorised multirange range uses its own MR-A through MR-E series. Always confirm which series covers the actual voltage set your work requires before ordering; it can't be changed after manufacture the way a simple accessory could.
</Callout>

<h2 id="when-it-pays-off">When Multirange Is Worth the Extra Cost</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Mixed-voltage maintenance work</strong>: Contractors and utility maintenance teams routinely testing LV, MV, and sometimes HV equipment in the same week get the clearest payoff — one instrument instead of two or three.</li>
  <li><strong>Limited storage or transport capacity</strong>: Field teams carrying test kit to remote sites benefit from consolidating multiple voltage capabilities into one case.</li>
  <li><strong>Commissioning teams testing varied equipment</strong>: EPC and substation commissioning work spans equipment from LV panels to HV switchgear on the same project — a multirange tester avoids swapping instruments between tasks.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="when-not-needed">When a Fixed-Voltage Tester Is the Better Choice</h2>

<p>
  A team that only ever tests one class of equipment — say, LV motors exclusively, always at 500 V — gains nothing from multirange capability and pays for flexibility it will never use. A fixed-voltage instrument at the single voltage actually needed is simpler, and there's no series-selection step to get right at order time.
</p>

